Subject: [PATCH v2 0/2] Archive messages that need no answer
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>

A message leaves the inbox only when you reply to it, so a thread
never closes. Someone always has the last word. This series adds a
single shared archive that the moderator manages: they move a
message's file, unchanged, into maildir/archive/, and notmuch new then
tags it archived and drops it from the inbox for everyone. Moving the
file back undoes it.

Patch 1 derives the tag in mail-tag.sh. Patch 2 documents archiving in
the workflow as a moderator step. Contributors never archive: their
commits still only add messages, and they answer everything in their
inbox.

Tested on a scratch copy of the list. Archiving clears inbox, the tag
survives a database rebuild, unarchiving restores inbox, and a replied
message keeps replied. In jj the move is a single rename, and two
people archiving the same message merge without conflict.

The thread "need some way to close the loop on threads" [1] chose a
shared archive dir over notmuch-git(1) for now.

Changes in v2:
- Only the moderator archives, per "Claude doesn't archive email" [2].
- Drop "claude: Let process-email archive what needs no answer", which
  had the process-email skill archive instead of replying.
- Keep the moderator's check that submitted commits only add messages,
  instead of relaxing it to allow archive moves.
- Say in mail-tag.sh and the commit messages that the moderator
  archives, not anyone.
